{:edb/db-before-t 1752 :edb/db-after-t 1753 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535065 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:03:10.742Z" 13194139535065 true] [17592186087462 1100 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116$." 13194139535065 false] [17592186087462 1100 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116.$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186087462 1145 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{2} + 9y^{2} - 100x - 18y &= 116 \\\\\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116 \\\\\n25[x^{2} - 4x] + 9[y^{2} - 2y] &= 116 \\\\\n25[(x - 2)^{2} - 4] + 9[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 116 + 100 + 9 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 225 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(9) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(25) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(3^{2}) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5 =10\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 =6\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1})$." 13194139535065 false] [17592186087462 1145 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-100x-18y & =116 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}-4x]+9[{y}^{2}-2y] & =116 \\\\ 25[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =116+100+9 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5 =10,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 3 =6,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1}).$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186087463 1100 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0$." 13194139535065 false] [17592186087463 1100 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0.$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186087463 1145 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - 6x + 24y + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + 4[y^{2} + 6y] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4[(y + 3)^{2} - 9] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 9 + 36 - 41 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 2 =4\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1 =2\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3})$." 13194139535065 false] [17592186087463 1145 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-6x+24y+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4[{y}^{2}+6y]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =9+36-41 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1 =2,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3}).$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186087663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329296 13194139535065 true] [17592186087663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329338 13194139535065 true] [17592186087663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329343 13194139535065 true] [17592186087663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329348 13194139535065 true] [17592186087663 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329355 13194139535065 true] [17592186087664 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329303 13194139535065 true] [17592186087664 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329310 13194139535065 true] [17592186087664 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329317 13194139535065 true] [17592186087664 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329324 13194139535065 true] [17592186087664 1020 #edb/ref 17592186329331 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1017 #edb/ref 17592186329297 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1089 "q-115314" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1100 "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y=-124.$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1113 #uuid "e8fdb3e7-9493-53f7-a6c7-da3464300fc3" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329296 1145 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y & =-124 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+12x]+4[{y}^{2}+8y] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+4[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-36]+4[(y+4)^{2}-16] & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =-124+108+64 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-6,-4).$" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329298 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329299 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329300 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329301 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1000 #edb/ref 17592186329302 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1041 "selection" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1104 #edb/ref 17592186329298 13194139535065 true] [17592186329297 1110 #uuid "28c0d1c1-846e-58ea-859c-cd4bd00a45dd" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329298 1034 "(-6,-4)" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535065 true] [17592186329298 1111 #uuid "908681d4-5f25-5951-889f-26e9e13164c8" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329299 1034 "(4,2\\sqrt{3})" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535065 true] [17592186329299 1111 #uuid "2ca17eca-d557-5b5d-971a-cb88d7aeccd3" 13194139535065 true] [17592186329300 1034 "(-4,-6)" 13194139535065